const USFM = `\\id HEB - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\\usfm 3.0
\\ide UTF-8
\\h Hebrews
\\toc1 The Word of Exhortation to the Hebrews
\\toc2 Hebrews
\\toc3 Heb
\\mt1 Hebrews

\\imt Introduction
\\ip The writer never gives his name, and the document does not open like a letter — no sender, no recipients, no greeting, only a sentence about God speaking. It ends like one, with a travel notice and greetings, but what lies between is a sermon. He says so himself at the close: it is a "word of encouragement" (13:22), which was the standing term for the address given in a synagogue after the reading of the Law and the Prophets. It was written to be read aloud to one congregation, in one sitting.
\\ip That congregation knew the Jewish scriptures from the inside. The whole argument runs on the Greek Old Testament and assumes hearers who can follow a chain of psalms without being told where the psalms come from, who know what the high priest did on the Day of Atonement and what every piece of furniture in the tent was for. The writer explains none of it. He also speaks of the priests as still offering (8:4) and of the old arrangement as aging and close to vanishing but not yet gone (8:13), which puts the writing before the Romans destroyed the temple in the year 70. Timothy is known to both sides, and the greetings come from people "from Italy," which points to Rome or its neighborhood as the destination.
\\ip These people had already paid for their confession once. After they first believed they were put on public display, insulted, imprisoned, and stripped of their property, and they took it with joy (10:32-34). That was years ago. Now they are tired. Some have stopped coming to the meetings (10:25). They have slid so far backward that they need the first things taught to them again (5:11-14). And the pressure on them has an obvious exit: they can drift back into the older, legal, unpersecuted form of their own religion — temple, priesthood, sacrifices, everything they were raised on, without Jesus.
\\ip Every argument in the sermon is built across that exit. The Son is greater than the angels who carried the Law, greater than Moses, greater than Joshua, greater than Aaron; his priesthood belongs to an order older than Aaron's and does not end at his death; his one sacrifice does what the annual ones never could. Going back would not be trading one good thing for another but trading the thing itself for the shadow it cast. Five times the argument breaks off into warning, each sharper than the last, because the writer believes his hearers can genuinely lose what they hold. And it ends with the hardest demand in the book: Jesus was executed outside the city wall, so go out to him there, and carry the disgrace.

\\c 1
\\s1 God Has Spoken to Us in a Son
\\p
\\v 1 In many pieces and in many ways God spoke long ago to the fathers in the prophets \\add [i.e. a piece at a time, and never the whole of it in any one of them]\\add*;
\\v 2 at the end of these days he has spoken to us in a Son \\add [— not in one more prophet, but in one who is Son]\\add*, whom he appointed heir of all things, through whom also he made the ages\\f + \\fr 1:2 \\ft Greek \\tl aiōnas\\tl*, "the ages" — time itself along with everything in it, the created order taken in its whole span rather than as so much matter.\\f*.
\\v 3 He is the shining out of his glory and the exact stamp of his very being\\f + \\fr 1:3 \\ft Greek \\tl charaktēr\\tl*, the mark a die leaves when it strikes a coin — not a likeness someone attempted, but the impression itself, exact because it was made by contact.\\f*, carrying all things along by the word of his power; and when through himself he had made cleansing for sins, he sat down at the right hand of the Majesty on high\\f + \\fr 1:3 \\ft "The Majesty" stands in for the divine name, which reverence kept out of ordinary speech; compare "the Power" in Mark 14:62. The posture matters as much as the title: no chair stood anywhere in the sanctuary, because a priest's work was never finished.\\f*,
\\v 4 having become as much better than the angels as the name \\add [i.e. Son]\\add* he has inherited stands above theirs.\\f + \\fr 1:4 \\ft Angels are not an idle comparison. Jewish tradition held that the Law was given at Sinai through angels — Deuteronomy 33:2 in the Greek, and see Acts 7:53 and Galatians 3:19; the writer says it himself at 2:2. Anyone thinking of going back to the Law without the Son would be trading the greater messenger for the lesser.\\f*
\\s1 No Angel Was Ever Called Son
\\p
\\v 5 For to which of the angels did he ever say, "You are my Son; today I have fathered you"? And again, "I will be a Father to him, and he will be a Son to me"?\\f + \\fr 1:5 \\ft Psalm 2:7 and 2 Samuel 7:14. Both belong to the making of a king in David's line: the "today" is the day of enthronement, when God publicly declared him son. Angels are called sons of God as a class (Job 1:6; 38:7), but God never turned to one of them and said, "You are my Son."\\f*
\\v 6 And again, when he brings the firstborn \\add [i.e. the son who holds the rank and the rights of the firstborn]\\add* into the inhabited world, he says, "And let all God's angels bow down to him."\\f + \\fr 1:6 \\ft Deuteronomy 32:43 in the Greek, which carries a line the Hebrew text does not; a Dead Sea scroll of Deuteronomy has it as well. Compare Psalm 97:7. "Firstborn" is a rank, not a birth order: God calls Israel his firstborn (Exodus 4:22) and says of David's heir, "I will make him firstborn, highest of the kings of the earth" (Psalm 89:27).\\f*
\\v 7 And of the angels he says, "He makes his angels winds, and his ministers a flame of fire."\\f + \\fr 1:7 \\ft Psalm 104:4. Wind and fire are made things, and they are sent: they shift, they flare up and go out, and they do what they are told. That is the point of quoting it here.\\f*
\\v 8 But of the Son: "Your throne, O God, is forever and ever, and the rod of uprightness is the rod of your kingdom.
\\v 9 You loved righteousness and hated lawlessness; therefore God, your God, anointed you with the oil of gladness beyond your companions."\\f + \\fr 1:9 \\ft Psalm 45:6-7, written for the wedding of an Israelite king. The Greek reads the opening as a direct address — "your throne, O God" — and then in the next breath says "God, your God," so that the one addressed as God has God over him. The writer takes both halves exactly as they stand.\\f*
\\v 10 And, "You, Lord, at the beginning laid the foundation of the earth, and the heavens are the works of your hands.
\\v 11 They will perish, but you remain; and they will all wear out like a garment,
\\v 12 and like a cloak you will roll them up, and like a garment they will be changed. But you are the same, and your years will not give out."\\f + \\fr 1:12 \\ft Psalm 102:25-27. In the psalm the one addressed is the LORD himself, and the speaker is a dying man setting his own few days against God's permanence. The writer hands the whole address to the Son.\\f*
\\v 13 But to which of the angels has he ever said, "Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies as a footstool for your feet"?\\f + \\fr 1:13 \\ft Psalm 110:1, the text the first believers quoted more than any other. The footstool comes from the way victory was pictured: the conquering king set his foot on the neck of the man he had beaten (Joshua 10:24). "Until" sets a horizon — he reigns now, and the last of the opposition is not yet down.\\f*
\\v 14 Are they not all spirits in service, sent out to serve for the sake of those who are about to inherit salvation \\add [— that is, for your sake]\\add*?

\\c 2
\\s1 So Do Not Let It Slide Past You
\\p
\\v 1 Because of this we must hold all the more closely to what we have heard, so that we are not carried past it\\f + \\fr 2:1 \\ft The picture is a ship. The verb is used of a vessel carried past its harbor by the current, which is exactly how the danger here works — nobody decides to drift.\\f*.
\\v 2 For if the word spoken through angels \\add [i.e. the Law given at Sinai]\\add* proved firm, and every stepping over it and every refusal to hear it received the wage it had coming,
\\v 3 how will we escape if we pay no attention to a salvation this great? It began by being spoken through the Lord, and it was made firm for us by those who heard him \\add [— we were not there ourselves; we had it from the men who were]\\add*,
\\v 4 while God bore witness alongside them with signs and wonders and every kind of powerful work and with distributions of the Holy Spirit \\add [i.e. gifts handed out among us]\\add* according to his will.
\\s1 Not Angels, but a Man — and We Do See Him
\\p
\\v 5 For it was not to angels that he put in subjection the inhabited world to come, which is what we are speaking about.
\\v 6 But someone has borne witness somewhere,\\f + \\fr 2:6 \\ft Psalm 8:4-6. He does not name David or number the psalm; who spoke it is beside the point, since God is the one speaking in it. The Hebrew says man was made "a little lower than God"; the Greek says "than the angels," and that is why this psalm belongs in an argument about angels.\\f* saying, "What is man, that you remember him, or a son of man, that you look after him?
\\v 7 You made him for a little while lower than the angels\\f + \\fr 2:7 \\ft The Greek can mean "a little lower" in rank or "for a little while" lower in time. In the psalm it is rank; at verse 9 the writer takes it as time.\\f*; you crowned him with glory and honor,
\\v 8 and you put everything under his feet." For in putting everything under him, he left nothing that is not under him. But as it is, we do not yet see everything put under him \\add [— look around: plainly we do not]\\add*.
\\v 9 But we do see Jesus \\add [— one man in whom it has actually happened]\\add*, who was made for a little while lower than the angels, crowned with glory and honor because of the suffering of death, so that by God's favor he might taste death\\f + \\fr 2:9 \\ft To taste is not to sip. In this Greek it means to go all the way into a thing and know it firsthand — which is how the same verb should be heard again at 6:4-5.\\f* for everyone.
\\s1 He Is Not Ashamed to Call Us Brothers
\\p
\\v 10 For it was fitting for him, for whom everything exists and through whom everything exists, in bringing many sons to glory, to bring the leader of their salvation to completion through sufferings.\\f + \\fr 2:10 \\ft Two words carry this sentence. "Leader" is \\tl archēgos\\tl*, the man who goes first and opens a road others will travel behind him. "Bring to completion" is \\tl teleioō\\tl*, which in the Greek Old Testament is used for consecrating a priest — filling his hands, finishing him for the office. It does not mean mending a fault. Nothing was wrong with him that suffering repaired; suffering is what qualified him.\\f*
\\v 11 For the one who makes holy and those who are made holy are all out of one \\add [i.e. one stock, one family]\\add*; and for this reason he is not ashamed to call them brothers,
\\v 12 saying, "I will tell your name to my brothers; in the middle of the assembly I will sing your praise."\\f + \\fr 2:12 \\ft Psalm 22:22. That psalm opens, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me," and runs through mockery, thirst, pierced hands and feet, and garments divided by lot — and then turns, at this verse, to a rescued man standing up in the congregation to say out loud what God has done.\\f*
\\v 13 And again, "I will put my trust in him." And again, "Here I am, and the children God gave me."\\f + \\fr 2:13 \\ft Isaiah 8:17-18. Isaiah spoke both lines while God's face was hidden from the nation and most of it was stumbling: he and his children stood as a small faithful company inside a people that had turned. The first line is the more startling of the two here — the Son is shown exercising trust, not merely receiving it.\\f*
\\s1 He Took Our Flesh in Order to Break Death and to Be Our Priest
\\p
\\v 14 Since, then, the children share blood and flesh, he too took his share of the same, so that through death he might put out of action the one who holds the power of death, that is, the devil,
\\v 15 and set free those who through fear of death were held in slavery all their lives.
\\v 16 For surely it is not angels he takes hold of; he takes hold of Abraham's seed.\\f + \\fr 2:16 \\ft The verb is the one used in the Greek Old Testament when God takes Israel by the hand — "you whom I took hold of from the ends of the earth" (Isaiah 41:9), "the day I took them by the hand to lead them out of Egypt" (Jeremiah 31:32). And it is Abraham's family he grips: not humanity in the abstract, but the people the promises were made to.\\f*
\\v 17 So he had to be made like his brothers in every way, so that he might become a merciful and faithful high priest in the things toward God, to cover over the sins of the people.\\f + \\fr 2:17 \\ft The verb belongs to the Day of Atonement, the one day in the year when the high priest went in with blood and made covering for the whole nation (Leviticus 16). This is the first time in the sermon Jesus is called high priest, and the title is not reached by a proof text. It is reached by the fact that he became one of us.\\f*
\\v 18 For since he himself has suffered when he was tested, he is able to help those who are being tested \\add [— you, now]\\add*.

\\c 3
\\s1 A Son over the House, Not a Servant in It
\\p
\\v 1 Therefore, holy brothers, sharers in a heavenly calling, look closely at the apostle\\f + \\fr 3:1 \\ft "One sent." This is the only place in the New Testament where the word is used of Jesus, and it is chosen for the comparison that follows: Moses too was a man God sent.\\f* and high priest of our confession \\add [i.e. of what we say together, out loud]\\add*, Jesus,
\\v 2 who was faithful to the one who appointed him, as Moses also was in all his house.\\f + \\fr 3:2 \\ft The words are God's own, spoken in Moses' defence when his authority was challenged: "my servant Moses; he is faithful in all my house" (Numbers 12:7). The writer does not chip away at Moses. He starts from God's estimate of him and argues from there.\\f*
\\v 3 For this one has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses, in the same measure as the one who builds a house has more honor than the house.
\\v 4 For every house is built by someone, but the one who built everything is God.
\\v 5 And Moses was faithful in all his house as a servant \\add [i.e. a trusted attendant of the household — an honor, not a slight]\\add*, for a witness to the things that were going to be spoken;
\\v 6 but Christ was faithful as a son over his house. And we are his house, if we hold on to our boldness \\add [i.e. our freedom to speak up and come near without cringing]\\add* and the boast of our hope.\\f + \\fr 3:6 \\ft The "if" is a live condition, not a turn of phrase, and the same sentence comes again in different words at verse 14. Everything from here through the end of chapter 4 is built on it.\\f*
\\s1 Do Not Harden Your Hearts, as They Did in the Wilderness
\\p
\\v 7 Therefore, as the Holy Spirit says, "Today, if you hear his voice,
\\v 8 do not harden your hearts as in the bitter quarrel, on the day of the testing in the wilderness,\\f + \\fr 3:8 \\ft Psalm 95:7-11. The Hebrew names two places, Meribah and Massah, where Israel quarrelled over water and put God to the test (Exodus 17:1-7). The Greek translates the names into what happened at them — the quarrel, the testing — so the warning stops being about a location and becomes about a way of behaving that any generation can repeat.\\f*
\\v 9 where your fathers tested me by putting me to the proof, and they saw my works
\\v 10 for forty years. Therefore I was angry with that generation, and I said, 'They always wander in their heart, and they have not known my ways.'
\\v 11 As I swore in my anger, 'They shall never enter into my rest.'"\\f + \\fr 3:11 \\ft The oath was sworn after the people, standing at the border, refused to go into the land: "your corpses shall fall in this wilderness" (Numbers 14:20-35). God forgave them when Moses interceded, and the oath still stood.\\f*
\\s1 Keep Each Other Awake While It Is Still Called Today
\\p
\\v 12 Watch out, brothers, that there will not be in any one of you an evil, unfaithful\\f + \\fr 3:12 \\ft Greek \\tl pistis\\tl* holds two things in one word: trust, and the loyalty that trust produces. English "faith" carries only the first half, so this translation says "faithfulness" throughout, and "unfaithful" here for its opposite.\\f* heart that stands away from the living God.
\\v 13 But encourage one another every day, as long as it is called "Today," \\add [— and that window will not stand open forever]\\add* so that no one among you is hardened by the deceit of sin.
\\v 14 For we have become sharers in the Christ, if indeed we hold firm to the end the ground we stood on at the start \\add [i.e. the confidence we began with]\\add*,
\\v 15 while it is said, "Today, if you hear his voice, do not harden your hearts as in the bitter quarrel."
\\s1 Who Was It Who Fell? The Ones Who Came Out of Egypt
\\p
\\v 16 For who were the ones who heard and rebelled? Was it not all those who came out of Egypt through Moses?
\\v 17 And with whom was he angry for forty years? Was it not with those who sinned, whose bodies fell in the wilderness?\\f + \\fr 3:17 \\ft These were people God had already rescued. They had walked through the sea, eaten the manna, watched the cloud and the fire for forty years. Coming out did not get them in.\\f*
\\v 18 And to whom did he swear that they would not enter into his rest, if not to those who disobeyed?
\\v 19 And we see that they were not able to enter because of unfaithfulness.

\\c 4
\\s1 The Rest Is Still Open
\\p
\\v 1 Let us fear, then, while the promise of entering into his rest still stands, that any one of you should be judged to have fallen short of it.
\\v 2 For we have had the good news brought to us just as they did; but the word they heard did them no good, because they were not joined by faithfulness to those who did hear.\\f + \\fr 4:2 \\ft The Greek of this last clause is awkward and the manuscripts differ over it. The sense it carries either way: hearing did them no good because the word never fused with trust. It went in and lay there inert.\\f*
\\v 3 For we who have trusted are entering into that rest, just as he has said, "As I swore in my anger, 'They shall never enter into my rest'" — although his works were finished from the founding of the world \\add [— which means the rest he speaks of has been standing ready since creation]\\add*.
\\v 4 For somewhere he has spoken about the seventh day like this: "And God rested on the seventh day from all his works."\\f + \\fr 4:4 \\ft Genesis 2:2. In Greek, "rested" there and "rest" in Psalm 95 are the same word, and that is the hinge of the whole argument: the rest he swore they would not enter is the rest he entered himself when the work was done.\\f*
\\v 5 And again in this place: "They shall never enter into my rest."
\\s1 God Set Another Day, and Called It Today
\\p
\\v 6 Since, then, it remains for some to enter into it, and those who first had the good news brought to them did not enter because of disobedience,
\\v 7 he again marks out a certain day, "Today," saying through David so long afterward, as has already been said, "Today, if you hear his voice, do not harden your hearts."
\\v 8 For if Joshua had given them rest, God would not have spoken afterward about another day.\\f + \\fr 4:8 \\ft In Greek, "Joshua" and "Jesus" are the same name, \\tl Iēsous\\tl*, and a hearer could not miss it. One \\tl Iēsous\\tl* brought them into the land, and centuries later God was still holding the door of the rest open — so the land was never the rest. Another \\tl Iēsous\\tl* has gone into the rest itself.\\f*
\\v 9 So then, a sabbath rest remains for the people of God.\\f + \\fr 4:9 \\ft The word changes here — not the "rest" he has been using but \\tl sabbatismos\\tl*, a sabbath-keeping, a word found almost nowhere else in Greek. What remains is not a stretch of country but a share in the rest God took when his work was finished.\\f*
\\v 10 For the one who has entered into his rest has himself rested from his works, as God did from his own.
\\v 11 Let us hurry, then, to enter into that rest \\add [— strange as it sounds to have to work at entering a rest]\\add*, so that no one falls by the same pattern of disobedience.
\\s1 Nothing Is Hidden from the One We Answer To
\\p
\\v 12 For the word of God\\f + \\fr 4:12 \\ft Not a general remark about scripture. "The word of God" here is the particular thing he has been quoting for two chapters — the promise of rest, the sworn exclusion, the "Today" — and his point is that no one hears it neutrally. It is doing something to you while you listen to it.\\f* is living and at work, sharper than any two-edged sword, cutting through to the dividing of self and spirit, of joints and marrow \\add [i.e. all the way in, where nothing is jointed tightly enough to keep it out]\\add*, and able to judge the thoughts and intentions of the heart.
\\v 13 And no creature is hidden from him; everything lies naked with its throat bared\\f + \\fr 4:13 \\ft The word is built on the word for "throat." It has been explained as the beast's head pulled back to bare the throat for the knife, and as a man's face forced up so that he has to meet your eyes. Either way, nothing is left covered.\\f* before the eyes of the one to whom we must give our account.
\\s1 Come to the Throne with Boldness
\\p
\\v 14 Since, then, we have a great high priest who has gone through the heavens \\add [i.e. all the way in, as the high priest went through the tent to the innermost room]\\add*, Jesus the Son of God, let us hold on to our confession.
\\v 15 For we do not have a high priest who is unable to feel with us in our weaknesses, but one who has been tested in every way as we are, apart from sin.
\\v 16 So let us come to the throne of favor\\f + \\fr 4:16 \\ft Greek \\tl charis\\tl*, a gift given freely with nothing owed back for it; older English says "grace." This translation says "favor" throughout. Note which word he attaches to the throne: he has just said that nothing is hidden from the one we answer to, and now calls that same seat a throne of favor.\\f* with boldness, so that we may receive mercy and find favor for help at the right time.

\\c 5
\\s1 What a High Priest Is, and How One Is Made
\\p
\\v 1 For every high priest is taken from among men and appointed on behalf of men in the things toward God, to bring gifts and sacrifices for sins.
\\v 2 He is able to deal gently\\f + \\fr 5:2 \\ft The verb occurs nowhere else in the New Testament. It was a philosopher's word for the measured middle: neither fury at the offender nor indifference to the offence.\\f* with those who do not know and are wandering off, since he himself is wrapped around with weakness,
\\v 3 and because of it he has to offer for sins on his own behalf as well as for the people.
\\v 4 And no one takes this honor on himself; he is called by God, just as Aaron was.\\f + \\fr 5:4 \\ft God named Aaron; Aaron did not put himself forward (Exodus 28:1). Israel remembered what came of grabbing at the office: the ground opened under Korah and his company (Numbers 16), and King Uzziah, who walked in to burn incense, walked out a leper and stayed one until he died (2 Chronicles 26:16-21).\\f*
\\s1 Christ Did Not Take the Office; God Gave It to Him
\\p
\\v 5 So also the Christ did not glorify himself to become a high priest, but the one who said to him, "You are my Son; today I have fathered you,"
\\v 6 as he also says in another place, "You are a priest forever, in the order of Melchizedek."\\f + \\fr 5:6 \\ft Psalm 110:4 — the fourth verse of the psalm whose first verse was quoted at 1:13. One psalm, one figure, and he is both seated at God's right hand and a priest forever. Under the Law that combination was impossible: kings came from Judah, priests from Levi, and no man held both.\\f*
\\v 7 In the days of his flesh he offered up prayers and pleadings\\f + \\fr 5:7 \\ft The word behind "pleadings" names the olive branch a suppliant held out when he had nothing left but the mercy of the man he was begging. The scene the hearers knew is the garden on the night he was arrested: "let this cup pass from me — yet not as I will, but as you will."\\f*, with loud crying and tears, to the one who was able to save him out of death \\add [— and he was able: not by keeping him from it, but by bringing him up out of it]\\add*, and he was heard because of his reverence.
\\v 8 Son though he was, he learned obedience from what he suffered;\\f + \\fr 5:8 \\ft Not that he had been disobedient and learned better. Obedience has a cost that can only be known by paying it, and he paid it.\\f*
\\v 9 and having been brought to completion, he became the source of everlasting salvation for all who obey him,
\\v 10 named by God a high priest in the order of Melchizedek.
\\s1 You Have Gone Back to Milk
\\p
\\v 11 About this we have much to say, and it is hard to explain, since you have become slow in the ears \\add [— you were not always]\\add*.
\\v 12 For though by this time you ought to be teachers, you need someone to teach you again the first elements of God's sayings; you have come to need milk, not solid food.
\\v 13 For everyone who lives on milk is unskilled in the word of righteousness, since he is an infant.
\\v 14 But solid food is for the grown, for those who through practice have had their senses trained to tell good from evil.

\\c 6
\\s1 Leave the Foundation and Build on It
\\p
\\v 1 So let us leave behind the beginning word about the Christ \\add [i.e. the first things you were taught — leave them the way a builder leaves a foundation, by building on it]\\add* and be carried on to what is complete, not laying again a foundation of turning back from dead works\\f + \\fr 6:1 \\ft "Dead works" are actions belonging to the country of death — whether sins that lead there, or rituals that handle death's defilement without ever settling it. The phrase returns at 9:14.\\f* and of faithfulness toward God,
\\v 2 of teaching about washings\\f + \\fr 6:2 \\ft Plural, and not the ordinary word for the community's own baptism. New believers coming out of Judaism had several kinds of washing to sort out: the purifications of the Law, the baptism John preached, and their own.\\f* and the laying on of hands, about the raising of the dead and everlasting judgment.
\\v 3 And this we will do, if God permits.
\\s1 Those Who Have Had All of It and Fall Away Cannot Be Started Over
\\p
\\v 4 For it is impossible, in the case of those who have once been given light, who have tasted the heavenly gift and have become sharers in the Holy Spirit,
\\v 5 and have tasted the good word of God and the powers of the age to come,\\f + \\fr 6:5 \\ft Every word in this list is a word he uses elsewhere for the real thing. "Given light" is what he says of these very hearers at 10:32. "Tasted" is what he said of Jesus tasting death at 2:9 — not sampling it. "Sharers" is what he calls them at 3:1 and 3:14. He is not describing people who merely looked like believers from the outside.\\f*
\\v 6 and have then fallen away, to make them new again to a turning back, crucifying the Son of God for themselves all over again \\add [i.e. taking their stand with the men who put him there, as though he had deserved it]\\add* and holding him up to public shame.\\f + \\fr 6:6 \\ft The impossibility is not that God runs short of mercy. It is that the means has been used up. God's last word was the Son (1:1-2); there is no further gift behind the Spirit and no later age than the age to come. A man who has had all of it and handed it back has nothing left to be brought back by. The wilderness works the same way: God forgave that generation (Numbers 14:20) and they still never entered, and when they changed their minds and marched up the hill anyway they were beaten back (Numbers 14:39-45).\\f*
\\v 7 For ground that drinks the rain that keeps falling on it, and brings forth a crop useful to those for whom it is worked, has a share in God's blessing.
\\v 8 But if it bears thorns and thistles, it is worthless and close to a curse \\add [— close to it, mind: not yet under it]\\add*, and its end is burning.\\f + \\fr 6:8 \\ft "Thorns and thistles" is what the ground brought forth when it was cursed (Genesis 3:18), and a plot given every advantage that yields nothing usable is Isaiah's vineyard (Isaiah 5:1-7). Notice that both fields in this picture get the same rain. Nothing was wrong with what came down on them.\\f*
\\s1 But We Expect Better of You
\\p
\\v 9 But about you, loved ones,\\f + \\fr 6:9 \\ft The only time in the whole sermon he calls them this. He has just said the hardest thing in the book, and he wants them to know who said it.\\f* we are persuaded of better things, things that go with salvation — even though we speak like this.
\\v 10 For God is not unjust; he will not forget your work and the love you showed for his name in having served the holy ones and in going on serving them \\add [— and you are serving them still; that is what persuades us]\\add*.
\\v 11 And we long for each one of you to show the same eagerness toward the full assurance of hope right to the end,
\\v 12 so that you do not become sluggish, but imitators of those who through faithfulness and long patience are inheriting the promises.
\\s1 God Swore by Himself, and the Oath Holds
\\p
\\v 13 For when God made his promise to Abraham, since he had no one greater to swear by, he swore by himself,
\\v 14 saying, "Blessing, I will bless you; and multiplying, I will multiply you."\\f + \\fr 6:14 \\ft Genesis 22:16-17, sworn on the mountain after Abraham had bound Isaac. The doubled verbs are a Hebrew way of leaning on a word: bless and bless again, multiply and multiply again.\\f*
\\v 15 And so, having waited with long patience, Abraham obtained the promise.
\\v 16 For men swear by someone greater, and the oath is the end of every dispute for them, making the matter firm.
\\v 17 In the same way God, wanting to show the heirs of the promise all the more clearly how unchangeable his purpose was, came in between with an oath,
\\v 18 so that through two unchangeable things \\add [i.e. the promise and the oath sworn over it]\\add*, in which it is impossible for God to lie, we who have fled for refuge\\f + \\fr 6:18 \\ft The verb is the one used for running to the cities of refuge, the towns God set aside where a man with blood on his hands was safe from the avenger so long as he stayed inside the walls (Numbers 35:6-28).\\f* might have strong encouragement to take hold of the hope set before us.
\\v 19 We have this as an anchor of the self, safe and firm, and it goes in behind the curtain,\\f + \\fr 6:19 \\ft An anchor is thrown down; this one is thrown up. The curtain is the one that hung before the innermost room of the tent, which nobody passed but the high priest, once a year, carrying blood (Exodus 26:33; Leviticus 16:2).\\f*
\\v 20 where Jesus has entered ahead of us\\f + \\fr 6:20 \\ft The word is used of the runner sent out in front of an army or a fleet — one who goes first precisely because others are coming behind him. That is the break with the old pattern: the high priest went in alone and the people stayed outside.\\f* on our behalf, having become a high priest forever in the order of Melchizedek.

\\c 7
\\s1 Who Melchizedek Was
\\p
\\v 1 For this Melchizedek, king of Salem, priest of God Most High, who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings and blessed him,\\f + \\fr 7:1 \\ft Genesis 14:18-20, and nowhere else in Israel's scriptures except Psalm 110:4. He walks into the story with no introduction, blesses Abram, takes a tenth of the spoil, and walks out of it again.\\f*
\\v 2 and to whom Abraham divided a tenth of everything — his name, first of all, means "king of righteousness," and then he is also king of Salem, that is, "king of peace."\\f + \\fr 7:2 \\ft Salem was taken to be Jerusalem; Psalm 76:2 puts God's dwelling "in Salem." The order matters to him: righteousness first, and peace after it.\\f*
\\v 3 Without father, without mother, without a line of descent, having neither beginning of days nor end of life, but made like the Son of God, he remains a priest for all time.\\f + \\fr 7:3 \\ft This is said about the record, not about the man's biology. Scripture gives Melchizedek no father, no mother, no genealogy, no birth and no death — and for a priest that silence is loud, because the Levitical priesthood ran entirely on documented descent, and men who could not produce their line were struck off the rolls (Ezra 2:61-63). Note also which way the likeness runs: Melchizedek was made to resemble the Son, not the other way about.\\f*
\\s1 Levi Paid the Tenth Through Abraham
\\p
\\v 4 See how great this man was, to whom Abraham the patriarch \\add [i.e. the father of us all]\\add* gave a tenth out of the best of the spoils.
\\v 5 Now those of the sons of Levi who receive the priesthood have a command in the law to take tithes from the people, that is, from their own brothers, even though these also have come out of Abraham's loins.
\\v 6 But this man, whose descent is not counted from them, took a tenth from Abraham and blessed the one who held the promises.
\\v 7 And beyond all argument, it is the lesser who is blessed by the greater.
\\v 8 And here mortal men receive tithes, but there it is one of whom it is testified that he lives.
\\v 9 And one might even say that Levi, who receives tithes, has himself paid tithes through Abraham,
\\v 10 for he was still in the loins of his father when Melchizedek met him.\\f + \\fr 7:10 \\ft "One might even say" is his signal that he knows he is pressing. The reasoning was ordinary in his world: what the head of a family does, the family not yet born does in him. So the tribe that would one day collect the tenth from all Israel had, in its own ancestor, already paid it to this man.\\f*
\\s1 A New Priesthood Means a New Law
\\p
\\v 11 Now if completion had come through the Levitical priesthood — for it was on the basis of it that the people were given the law — what further need was there for another priest to arise in the order of Melchizedek, and not to be named in the order of Aaron?\\f + \\fr 7:11 \\ft "Completion" is his word for the whole point of having a priesthood: getting people all the way in to God. Verse 19 says plainly what he means by it — a better hope, "through which we come near to God." The argument is simply that God would not have announced another priest of another order if the first order had been finishing the work.\\f*
\\v 12 For when the priesthood is changed, a change of law necessarily comes with it.
\\v 13 For the one these things are said about belonged to another tribe, and no one from that tribe has ever served at the altar.
\\v 14 For it is plain that our Lord has sprung from Judah, and about that tribe Moses said nothing concerning priests.
\\s1 A Priest by the Power of a Life That Cannot Be Broken Down
\\p
\\v 15 And it is clearer still if another priest arises in the likeness of Melchizedek,
\\v 16 who has become a priest not by a law of fleshly command \\add [i.e. not by who his father was and how sound his body]\\add* but by the power of a life that cannot be broken down.
\\v 17 For it is testified, "You are a priest forever, in the order of Melchizedek."
\\v 18 So on the one hand a former command is set aside\\f + \\fr 7:18 \\ft A legal word: the formal cancelling of an earlier enactment. He does not say the command was wrong. He says it was weak — it could not do the one thing a priesthood exists to do.\\f* because of its weakness and uselessness,
\\v 19 for the law brought nothing to completion; and on the other a better hope is brought in, through which we come near to God.
\\s1 This One Was Made Priest by an Oath
\\p
\\v 20 And it did not happen without an oath. Those men became priests without any oath,
\\v 21 but this one with an oath, through the one who said to him, "The Lord has sworn and will not change his mind: 'You are a priest forever.'"\\f + \\fr 7:21 \\ft Psalm 110:4 again. Aaron's line was set up by command, and God swore nothing over it. Nothing in the Law says of any Levite, "the Lord has sworn and will not change his mind."\\f*
\\v 22 By so much has Jesus become the one who stands surety for a better covenant.\\f + \\fr 7:22 \\ft "Stands surety" renders a commercial word found nowhere else in the New Testament: the man who puts his own person behind someone else's obligation and makes it good if it fails. This is also the first time in the sermon the word "covenant" appears.\\f*
\\s1 He Never Hands the Office On
\\p
\\v 23 And those priests were many in number, because death kept them from staying on;
\\v 24 but he, because he remains forever, holds his priesthood without passing it on.\\f + \\fr 7:24 \\ft The Greek word can mean unchangeable or non-transferable, and both fit. The old line needed many men because each one died; this one needs no successor.\\f*
\\v 25 That is why he is able to save, all the way to the end, those who come to God through him, since he always lives to plead on their behalf.\\f + \\fr 7:25 \\ft The phrase carries both senses at once: completely, and without a gap in the coverage. There is never a moment when the office stands empty. And note how the ones saved are described — "those who come," a verb of continuing action, people who keep on coming.\\f*
\\s1 The High Priest We Needed
\\p
\\v 26 For such a high priest was fitting for us: holy, without evil, unstained, set apart from sinners \\add [i.e. not held off from them, but untouched by what they are]\\add*, and lifted higher than the heavens.
\\v 27 He does not need, as those high priests do, to bring sacrifices day after day, first for his own sins and then for those of the people; for he did this once for all when he offered himself.
\\v 28 For the law appoints men as high priests who have weakness, but the word of the oath, which came after the law, appoints a Son brought to completion forever.\\f + \\fr 7:28 \\ft The timing is part of the argument. Moses came first; the oath of Psalm 110 was spoken through David centuries later. The later word is the fuller one.\\f*

\\c 8
\\s1 The Main Point: Our Priest Serves in the True Tent
\\p
\\v 1 Now the main point in what we are saying is this: we have such a high priest, one who sat down \\add [— sat: the work was finished]\\add* at the right hand of the throne of the Majesty in the heavens,
\\v 2 a servant of the holy places and of the true tent, which the Lord pitched, not man.\\f + \\fr 8:2 \\ft "True" here does not mean real as against imaginary. The tent Moses built was real enough. It means original as against copy: this is the thing the other one was made from.\\f*
\\v 3 For every high priest is appointed to bring gifts and sacrifices; so this one too had to have something to bring.
\\v 4 Now if he were on earth, he would not be a priest at all \\add [i.e. the Law's arrangement leaves no opening for him]\\add*, since there are those who bring the gifts according to the law.\\f + \\fr 8:4 \\ft He writes as though the offerings are still going on, and at verse 13 as though the old arrangement is aging toward its end but not yet gone. That places the sermon before the Romans destroyed the temple in the year 70.\\f*
\\v 5 They serve a copy and shadow of the heavenly things, just as Moses was warned when he was about to complete the tent: "See that you make everything according to the pattern that was shown you on the mountain."\\f + \\fr 8:5 \\ft Exodus 25:40. The Law itself says the tent was built to a pattern Moses was shown — which makes the tent, on the Law's own testimony, a copy of something that already existed.\\f*
\\v 6 But as it is, he has obtained a service that is as much better as the covenant he mediates is better, since it has been enacted on better promises.
\\s1 God Himself Announced a New Covenant
\\p
\\v 7 For if that first covenant had been faultless, no place would have been sought for a second.
\\v 8 For finding fault with them he says, "Look, days are coming, says the Lord, when I will complete a new covenant with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah,\\f + \\fr 8:8 \\ft Jeremiah 31:31-34 — the longest quotation from the older scriptures anywhere in the New Testament, and he gives all of it. Jeremiah spoke it while Jerusalem was falling, the temple about to burn and the people about to be marched to Babylon. It is God's own announcement that what was made at Sinai would be replaced. Notice too where the fault is laid: on them, not on the covenant. But a covenant that could not cure their fault is a covenant that needed replacing, not patching.\\f*
\\v 9 not like the covenant I made with their fathers on the day I took hold of their hand to lead them out of the land of Egypt; because they did not remain in my covenant, and I turned away from them, says the Lord.\\f + \\fr 8:9 \\ft The Hebrew reads "though I was a husband to them"; the Greek he is quoting reads "and I turned away from them." In the Greek the breakdown runs both ways, which is what makes replacement rather than repair the only road left.\\f*
\\v 10 For this is the covenant I will make with the house of Israel after those days, says the Lord: I will put my laws into their mind, and I will write them on their hearts; and I will be their God, and they will be my people.\\f + \\fr 8:10 \\ft At Sinai the law was cut into stone and stood outside the people (Exodus 31:18). Here it is put inside them. That is the difference the whole quotation turns on: the first covenant could command obedience and could not produce it. Ezekiel, prophesying in the same catastrophe, said it the other way round — a new heart, and God's Spirit in them (Ezekiel 36:26-27).\\f*
\\v 11 And they will no longer teach each one his fellow citizen and each one his brother, saying, 'Know the Lord,' because they will all know me, from the least of them to the greatest of them.
\\v 12 For I will be merciful toward their wrongdoings, and I will remember their sins no more."\\f + \\fr 8:12 \\ft In covenant speech, for God to remember a sin is to act on it — to bring it up, to hold it against a man, to require something further for it. Not to remember it is to treat the case as closed.\\f*
\\v 13 In saying "new," he has made the first one old; and what is growing old and aging is close to vanishing.

\\c 9
\\s1 How the First Tent Was Arranged
\\p
\\v 1 Now the first covenant also had regulations for service and its holy place, an earthly one.
\\v 2 For a tent was prepared, the first one, in which were the lampstand and the table and the setting out of the loaves \\add [i.e. twelve of them, one for each tribe, kept before God]\\add*; this is called the Holy Place.
\\v 3 And behind the second curtain was a tent called the Holy of Holies,
\\v 4 having the golden censer\\f + \\fr 9:4 \\ft Greek \\tl thymiatērion\\tl*, the hand censer of coals — what the high priest carried in through the curtain on the Day of Atonement so that the cloud of incense would cover the ark (Leviticus 16:12-13). Most English translations render it "the altar of incense," but that altar stood in the outer room and not behind the curtain (Exodus 30:6; 40:26), and the Greek Old Testament calls it by a different name, \\tl thysiastērion thymiamatos\\tl*. He is listing what belongs to the inner room, and the censer is what belongs there.\\f* and the ark of the covenant covered all over with gold, in which were a golden jar holding the manna, and Aaron's staff that budded, and the tablets of the covenant;\\f + \\fr 9:4 \\ft Three objects, and each one a piece of the story: the jar of manna from the wilderness feeding (Exodus 16:33-34), the dead staff that blossomed when Aaron's priesthood was challenged (Numbers 17:1-11), and the stones God wrote on (Exodus 31:18). God's provision, God's choice of priest, God's terms.\\f*
\\v 5 and above it the cherubim of glory overshadowing the atonement cover.\\f + \\fr 9:5 \\ft The gold lid of the ark, where the blood was flicked on the Day of Atonement (Leviticus 16:14-15) and where God had said, "there I will meet with you" (Exodus 25:22).\\f* About these things we cannot now speak piece by piece.
\\s1 What the Arrangement Was Saying
\\p
\\v 6 With these things prepared like this, the priests go continually into the first tent, carrying out the acts of service;
\\v 7 but into the second only the high priest goes, once a year, and not without blood, which he brings for himself and for the sins the people committed in ignorance.\\f + \\fr 9:7 \\ft The qualification is the Law's own. Offerings covered sin done unwittingly; for the sin done deliberately, "with a high hand," the Law provided no sacrifice at all — that man was cut off from his people (Numbers 15:30-31). Keep it in mind for 10:26.\\f*
\\v 8 By this the Holy Spirit shows that the way into the holy places has not yet been made plain while the first tent is still standing \\add [i.e. as long as that arrangement stands, the road stays shut]\\add*.
\\v 9 This is a picture pointing to the present time, in which gifts and sacrifices are brought that cannot bring the one who serves to completion in his conscience \\add [i.e. cannot settle him on the inside]\\add*;
\\v 10 they deal only with food and drink and various washings, regulations for the flesh, laid on until the time of setting things straight.
\\s1 Christ Went In Once, with His Own Blood
\\p
\\v 11 But Christ came as high priest of the good things that have come, through the greater and more complete tent, not made with hands\\f + \\fr 9:11 \\ft "Made with hands" is the Greek Old Testament's standard sneer at idols — the gods men manufacture (Isaiah 2:18; 19:1; Leviticus 26:1). He turns it on the one sanctuary God had actually commanded, not to insult it but to place it: even that was built.\\f*, that is, not of this creation;
\\v 12 and not through the blood of goats and calves but through his own blood, he entered once for all into the holy places, having found an everlasting ransoming.\\f + \\fr 9:12 \\ft "Ransoming" belongs to the vocabulary of the exodus: the price paid to get a slave out, and what God did when he brought Israel out of Egypt (Exodus 6:6; Deuteronomy 7:8). It carries both the release and the cost of it.\\f*
\\v 13 For if the blood of goats and bulls and the ashes of a heifer, sprinkled on those who have been defiled, makes them holy so that their flesh is clean,\\f + \\fr 9:13 \\ft The red heifer of Numbers 19: burned whole, its ashes stirred into water, and that water sprinkled on anyone who had touched a corpse. It was the ordinary remedy for the commonest defilement there was, and it worked on the body.\\f*
\\v 14 how much more will the blood of the Christ, who through an everlasting spirit offered himself without blemish to God, cleanse our conscience from dead works to serve the living God.\\f + \\fr 9:14 \\ft "Without blemish" is the requirement laid on every animal brought to the altar (Exodus 12:5; Leviticus 1:3). Here the priest and the offering are the same person, and what the requirement asks for is not soundness of body.\\f*
\\s1 A Covenant Takes Effect by a Death
\\p
\\v 15 And for this reason he is the mediator of a new covenant, so that, a death having taken place to set them free from the transgressions committed under the first covenant \\add [— the ones the old sacrifices could only keep covering]\\add*, those who have been called might receive the promise of the everlasting inheritance.
\\v 16 For where there is a covenant, the death of the one who made it must be brought forward.\\f + \\fr 9:16 \\ft One Greek word, \\tl diathēkē\\tl*, covers both a covenant cut between parties and a will disposing of a man's estate, and he uses both senses at once here. A covenant is made firm over a death; a will does nothing whatever until the one who made it has died.\\f*
\\v 17 For a covenant is made firm over dead bodies, since it is never in force while the one who made it is alive.
\\v 18 That is why not even the first covenant was inaugurated without blood.
\\v 19 For when Moses had spoken every command of the law to all the people, he took the blood of calves and goats with water and scarlet wool and hyssop, and sprinkled the scroll itself and all the people,\\f + \\fr 9:19 \\ft Exodus 24:3-8, filled out with details drawn from the other cleansing rites — the water, the scarlet wool and the hyssop belong to the cleansing of a leper and to the red heifer (Leviticus 14:4-6; Numbers 19:6). He is describing the whole practice of covenant-making by blood, not only the one scene.\\f*
\\v 20 saying, "This is the blood of the covenant that God commanded for you."\\f + \\fr 9:20 \\ft Exodus 24:8. Anyone who had heard the account of the last supper would recognize the sentence at once: "This is my blood of the covenant, poured out for many."\\f*
\\v 21 And in the same way he sprinkled with blood both the tent and all the vessels of service.
\\v 22 And under the law almost everything is cleansed with blood, and without the pouring out of blood there is no release.\\f + \\fr 9:22 \\ft "Almost" is exact: a man too poor for an animal or birds could bring flour (Leviticus 5:11-13), and some things were purified by fire and water (Numbers 31:22-23). The rule behind the rest is Leviticus 17:11 — the life is in the blood, and it is the life that makes the covering.\\f*
\\s1 Once, at the Completion of the Ages
\\p
\\v 23 So it was necessary for the copies of the things in the heavens to be cleansed with these, but the heavenly things themselves with better sacrifices than these.\\f + \\fr 9:23 \\ft The plural "sacrifices" answers the plural "these," and is not a hint that he offered more than once; the whole sermon says otherwise. As for why the heavenly things should need anything done to them at all, he does not say. The logic he is following is that if the copy had to be consecrated with blood before it could be used, so must the original.\\f*
\\v 24 For Christ did not enter holy places made with hands, copies of the true ones, but into heaven itself, now to appear before the face of God for us.
\\v 25 Nor did he go in to offer himself over and over, as the high priest goes into the holy places year after year with blood not his own;
\\v 26 since then he would have had to suffer over and over from the founding of the world. But as it is, he has been made visible once, at the completion of the ages, to put sin away by the sacrifice of himself.
\\v 27 And just as it is laid up for men to die once, and after this judgment,
\\v 28 so the Christ, offered once to carry the sins of many, will appear a second time, apart from sin, to those who are waiting for him — for salvation.\\f + \\fr 9:28 \\ft "To carry the sins of many" is the language of Isaiah 53:12, the servant who bears what belonged to other people. And note who the second appearing brings salvation to: not those who once decided something, but "those who are waiting for him" — the ones still watching when he comes.\\f*

\\c 10
\\s1 The Same Sacrifices, Year After Year, Settle Nothing
\\p
\\v 1 For the law, having a shadow of the good things to come and not the very form of them \\add [i.e. the outline a thing throws, not the thing]\\add*, can never, by the same sacrifices they bring year after year without end, bring to completion those who come near.
\\v 2 Otherwise, would they not have stopped being brought, since those who serve, once cleansed, would no longer have any awareness of sins?
\\v 3 But in these sacrifices there is a reminder of sins year after year \\add [— every year the ritual says the matter is still open]\\add*.
\\v 4 For it is impossible for the blood of bulls and goats to take sins away.
\\s1 A Body You Fitted for Me
\\p
\\v 5 That is why, when he comes into the world, he says, "Sacrifice and offering you did not want, but you fitted a body for me.\\f + \\fr 10:5 \\ft Psalm 40:6-8. The Hebrew reads "ears you have dug for me"; the Greek he is quoting reads "a body you fitted for me." The prophets had been saying the same thing in their own way for centuries: "to obey is better than sacrifice" (1 Samuel 15:22), "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6), "I hate, I reject your festivals" (Amos 5:21).\\f*
\\v 6 In whole burnt offerings and offerings for sin you took no pleasure.
\\v 7 Then I said, 'Look, I have come — in the scroll of the book it is written about me — to do your will, O God.'"
\\v 8 Saying above, "Sacrifices and offerings and whole burnt offerings and offerings for sin you did not want and took no pleasure in" — and these are brought according to the law —
\\v 9 he then said, "Look, I have come to do your will." He takes away the first in order to set up the second \\add [i.e. the sacrifices go, and the doing of God's will stands in their place]\\add*.
\\v 10 And by that will we have been made holy through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all.
\\s1 One Sacrifice, and Then He Sat Down
\\p
\\v 11 And every priest stands day after day serving and bringing the same sacrifices over and over, which can never take sins away.
\\v 12 But this one, having brought one sacrifice for sins for all time, sat down at the right hand of God,\\f + \\fr 10:12 \\ft There were no seats in the sanctuary. A priest stood because he was never finished. This one sat down.\\f*
\\v 13 from then on waiting until his enemies are put as a footstool for his feet.
\\v 14 For by one offering he has brought to completion for all time those who are being made holy.\\f + \\fr 10:14 \\ft The two tenses are deliberate and they are not the same. The completing is finished and permanent. The making holy is going on now, in people who are still in the middle of it.\\f*
\\v 15 And the Holy Spirit also testifies to us; for after saying,
\\v 16 "This is the covenant I will make with them after those days, says the Lord: I will put my laws on their hearts, and I will write them on their mind,"
\\v 17 he then says, "And their sins and their lawless acts I will remember no more."
\\v 18 And where there is release of these, there is no longer an offering for sin.
\\s1 So Come In — and Hold On, and Keep Meeting
\\p
\\v 19 So then, brothers, since we have boldness to enter the holy places by the blood of Jesus,
\\v 20 by the new and living way he opened for us through the curtain, that is, his flesh,\\f + \\fr 10:20 \\ft The curtain was the barrier: it is what kept everyone out of the innermost room. He is saying the road through it ran through a body handed over to death — his own. The word for "new" here is built on a root meaning freshly killed; by this period it simply meant recent, but these were people who used sacrificial words every day.\\f*
\\v 21 and since we have a great priest over the house of God,
\\v 22 let us come near with a true heart in full assurance of faithfulness, our hearts sprinkled clean from an evil conscience and our bodies washed with clean water.\\f + \\fr 10:22 \\ft Both halves are priestly. Moses flung the covenant blood over the people at Sinai (Exodus 24:8), and no priest could serve until he had been washed with water at the door of the tent (Exodus 29:4). He is describing his hearers as men being made ready to go in and serve.\\f*
\\v 23 Let us hold on to the confession of our hope without bending, for the one who promised is faithful.
\\v 24 And let us keep watching one another closely, to stir each other up to love and good works,
\\v 25 not staying away from our own gathering, as some are in the habit of doing, but encouraging one another — and all the more as you see the Day \\add [i.e. the day he comes]\\add* coming near.
\\s1 If You Throw This Away, Nothing Is Left
\\p
\\v 26 For if we go on sinning willingly after receiving the full knowledge of the truth, there is no longer any sacrifice left for sins,\\f + \\fr 10:26 \\ft The Law itself drew this line. It provided offerings for sin done unwittingly and none at all for sin done deliberately, "with a high hand"; for that a man was cut off from his people (Numbers 15:30-31). The same line is drawn here. For someone who knows exactly what he is handing back, there is nothing further on offer — because there is nothing further.\\f*
\\v 27 but only a fearful expectation of judgment and a burning fury about to eat up those who set themselves against him.
\\v 28 Anyone who set aside Moses' law dies without pity on the word of two or three witnesses.\\f + \\fr 10:28 \\ft Deuteronomy 17:2-7: the covenant-breaker was executed on the evidence of two or three witnesses, and no clemency was provided for.\\f*
\\v 29 How much worse a punishment do you think will be counted right for the one who has trampled the Son of God underfoot, and treated as common \\add [i.e. as if it were any other blood]\\add* the blood of the covenant by which he was made holy, and insulted the Spirit of favor?\\f + \\fr 10:29 \\ft Notice what is said about the man: the blood he now calls common is the blood "by which he was made holy." The writer never softens a warning by suggesting such people were never really in. The whole weight of it depends on their having been.\\f*
\\v 30 For we know the one who said, "Vengeance is mine, I will repay," and again, "The Lord will judge his people."\\f + \\fr 10:30 \\ft Deuteronomy 32:35-36, from the Song of Moses. Both lines are spoken about God's own people, not about foreign nations — which is exactly why he quotes them here.\\f*
\\v 31 It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.
\\s1 Remember What You Already Endured
\\p
\\v 32 But remember the earlier days, when after you had been given light \\add [i.e. when you first believed]\\add* you stood your ground in a hard struggle with sufferings,
\\v 33 sometimes put on public show under insults and troubles, and sometimes standing with those who were treated this way.
\\v 34 For you suffered along with the prisoners, and you took the seizing of your possessions with joy, knowing that you yourselves have a better possession, and one that stays.
\\v 35 So do not throw away your boldness, which carries a great wage with it.
\\v 36 For you need endurance, so that when you have done the will of God you may receive what was promised.
\\v 37 For yet a very little while, and "the one who is coming will come and will not delay.\\f + \\fr 10:37 \\ft The opening phrase is Isaiah 26:20; the rest is Habakkuk 2:3-4. In Habakkuk it is the vision that comes; the Greek turns it into a person, "the one who is coming." He also reverses the order of the two halves, so that living by faithfulness comes first and the shrinking back second.\\f*
\\v 38 But my righteous one will live by faithfulness, and if he shrinks back, I take no pleasure in him."
\\v 39 But we are not of those who shrink back to ruin, but of those who are faithful, to the keeping of the self.

\\c 11
\\s1 What Faithfulness Is
\\p
\\v 1 Now faithfulness is the ground under things hoped for, the proof of things not seen.\\f + \\fr 11:1 \\ft Not a dictionary entry but a working description of what the rest of the chapter will show. The first word is what stands underneath a thing and holds it up; the second is the evidence that settles a question. Faithfulness is what makes an unseen thing solid enough to act on.\\f*
\\v 2 For by it the elders were given their witness \\add [i.e. God himself spoke well of them]\\add*.
\\v 3 By faithfulness we understand that the ages were put in order by the word of God, so that what is seen has not come into being out of what appears \\add [— the visible world came out of the invisible; that is the pattern everything in this chapter runs on]\\add*.
\\s1 Abel, Enoch, Noah
\\p
\\v 4 By faithfulness Abel brought God a better sacrifice than Cain, and through it he was given witness that he was righteous, God bearing witness over his gifts; and through it, though he died, he still speaks.\\f + \\fr 11:4 \\ft Genesis 4:3-10. Genesis never says why God had regard for Abel's offering and not for Cain's; the writer supplies the reason. And Abel still speaks because his blood cried out from the ground after Cain had killed him — a point he will pick up again at 12:24.\\f*
\\v 5 By faithfulness Enoch was taken up so that he did not see death, and he was not found, because God took him up; for before he was taken up he was given witness that he had pleased God.\\f + \\fr 11:5 \\ft Genesis 5:24. Every other name in that long list of generations ends the same way — "and he died." Enoch's does not: "he was not, for God took him."\\f*
\\v 6 And apart from faithfulness it is impossible to please him, for whoever comes to God must trust that he is, and that he pays back those who search him out.
\\v 7 By faithfulness Noah, warned about things not yet seen, in reverent fear built an ark for the saving of his household; through it he condemned the world and became an heir of the righteousness that comes by faithfulness.
\\s1 Abraham and Sarah: Living in Tents on a Promise
\\p
\\v 8 By faithfulness Abraham obeyed when he was called to go out to a place he was going to receive as an inheritance, and he went out without knowing where he was going.
\\v 9 By faithfulness he lived as a stranger in the land of the promise, as in a land not his own, living in tents with Isaac and Jacob, heirs with him of the same promise.
\\v 10 For he was waiting for the city that has foundations \\add [i.e. the opposite of a tent]\\add*, whose builder and maker is God.
\\v 11 By faithfulness Sarah herself, barren as she was, received power for the laying down of seed even past the season of her age, since she counted the one who promised faithful.\\f + \\fr 11:11 \\ft The Greek here is difficult: the phrase about the laying down of seed is normally used of the man's part, and some read Abraham as the subject with Sarah alongside him. Either way Sarah is the one credited with counting the promiser faithful. Genesis also records that she laughed when she first heard it and then denied laughing (Genesis 18:12-15). He records where she came out, not every step on the way.\\f*
\\v 12 And so from one man, and him as good as dead, there were born descendants as many as the stars of heaven and as countless as the sand along the edge of the sea.
\\s1 They Died Still Reaching, and Called Themselves Strangers
\\p
\\v 13 All these died in faithfulness, not having received the promises but having seen them from far off and greeted them \\add [i.e. waved across the distance, the way you hail a man still far down the road]\\add*, and having confessed that they were strangers and sojourners on the earth.\\f + \\fr 11:13 \\ft Not a pious figure of speech. It was their legal standing and they said so: "I am a stranger and a sojourner among you" (Abraham, Genesis 23:4); "the years of my sojourning" (Jacob to Pharaoh, Genesis 47:9). They owned nothing in the land but a grave.\\f*
\\v 14 For those who say such things make it plain that they are searching for a homeland of their own.
\\v 15 And if they had been remembering the one they came out of, they would have had time to turn back \\add [— the road home was open the whole time, and they did not take it]\\add*.
\\v 16 But as it is, they reach for a better one, that is, a heavenly one. That is why God is not ashamed of them, to be called their God; for he has prepared a city for them.\\f + \\fr 11:16 \\ft "The God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, the God of Jacob" (Exodus 3:6) — he tied his own name to theirs. That is what "not ashamed" means here.\\f*
\\s1 Isaac, Jacob, Joseph: Blessing What They Would Never See
\\p
\\v 17 By faithfulness Abraham, when he was tested, offered up Isaac; the one who had received the promises was offering up his only son,
\\v 18 the one about whom it had been said, "In Isaac your seed will be named."
\\v 19 He reckoned that God is able even to raise from the dead; and from there, in a figure, he did get him back.\\f + \\fr 11:19 \\ft The two commands came from the same God: the promise runs through Isaac, and Isaac is to be sacrificed. The writer says Abraham resolved it the only way it can be resolved — God can raise the dead. And Isaac came back off the altar alive, which is that resolution acted out in figure.\\f*
\\v 20 By faithfulness Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau concerning things to come.
\\v 21 By faithfulness Jacob, as he was dying, blessed each of Joseph's sons and bowed in worship over the top of his staff.\\f + \\fr 11:21 \\ft Genesis 47:31. The Hebrew has him bowing at the head of his bed; the Greek reads the same consonants as "staff." A dying man, propped on a stick, handing out a future he will not live to see.\\f*
\\v 22 By faithfulness Joseph, at the end of his life, remembered the going out of the sons of Israel and gave orders about his bones \\add [i.e. he would not be left buried in Egypt]\\add*.
\\s1 Moses Chose the Reproach over Egypt
\\p
\\v 23 By faithfulness Moses, when he was born, was hidden for three months by his parents, because they saw that the child was beautiful, and they did not fear the king's order.
\\v 24 By faithfulness Moses, when he had grown up, refused to be called the son of Pharaoh's daughter,
\\v 25 choosing rather to be badly treated along with the people of God than to have the passing enjoyment of sin,
\\v 26 counting the reproach of the Christ greater riches than the treasures of Egypt, for he was looking away toward the wage.\\f + \\fr 11:26 \\ft The disgrace that falls on God's people is the disgrace of God's anointed, in whatever century it falls: "the reproach with which they have reproached the footsteps of your anointed" (Psalm 89:50-51). It is also the same word he used at 10:33 for what these hearers had already taken.\\f*
\\v 27 By faithfulness he left Egypt, not fearing the king's anger, for he held on as though seeing the one who is unseen.
\\v 28 By faithfulness he kept the Passover and the pouring of the blood, so that the destroyer of the firstborn would not touch them.
\\v 29 By faithfulness they went through the Red Sea as through dry land; and when the Egyptians tried it, they were swallowed up \\add [— the same water, and it depended entirely on who had been told to walk into it]\\add*.
\\v 30 By faithfulness the walls of Jericho fell down after they had been circled for seven days.
\\v 31 By faithfulness Rahab the prostitute \\add [— a Canaanite, with no part in the covenant at all]\\add* did not die along with those who disobeyed, since she had welcomed the spies in peace.
\\s1 Some Conquered; Others Were Sawn in Two
\\p
\\v 32 And what more shall I say? Time will run out on me if I go on telling of Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, and of David and Samuel and the prophets,
\\v 33 who through faithfulness conquered kingdoms, worked righteousness, obtained promises, shut the mouths of lions,
\\v 34 put out the power of fire, escaped the edge of the sword, were made strong out of weakness, became mighty in war, turned back the armies of foreigners.\\f + \\fr 11:34 \\ft Daniel in the pit of lions (Daniel 6), his three friends in the furnace (Daniel 3), and behind the rest the whole run of Israel's deliverances. He does not stop to name them; his hearers could.\\f*
\\v 35 Women received their dead back by resurrection. And others were beaten to death, refusing the release they were offered, so that they might obtain a better resurrection.\\f + \\fr 11:35 \\ft The turn in the middle of this verse is the point of the whole list. The first half is Elijah and Elisha giving sons back to their mothers (1 Kings 17; 2 Kings 4). The second is the martyrs under Antiochus, an old scribe and a mother with seven sons, offered their lives back for one act of compliance and refusing it one after another (2 Maccabees 6-7). Both halves are faithfulness. The difference was not in them.\\f*
\\v 36 And others had a taste of mockings and floggings, and of chains and prison as well.
\\v 37 They were stoned, they were sawn in two, they died by murder with the sword; they went about in sheepskins, in goatskins, in need, pressed hard, badly treated —\\f + \\fr 11:37 \\ft Zechariah son of Jehoiada was stoned in the temple court (2 Chronicles 24:20-21); Jewish tradition held that Isaiah was sawn in half under Manasseh; Uriah the prophet was cut down by the sword on the king's order (Jeremiah 26:23). The skins are the prophet's clothing — Elijah's hair garment (2 Kings 1:8).\\f*
\\v 38 the world was not worthy of them — wandering in deserts and mountains and caves and holes in the ground.
\\s1 None of Them Received It — They Were Waiting for Us
\\p
\\v 39 And all these, given their witness through faithfulness, did not receive what was promised,
\\v 40 since God had foreseen something better for us, so that they would not be brought to completion apart from us.\\f + \\fr 11:40 \\ft Not that God preferred us to them. What they were waiting for could not arrive until the Son came, the offering was made and the way in was opened — and that happened in our lifetime, not theirs. They are not finished without us because the thing that finishes them is the thing we have been handed. At 12:23 he says it is now done: the righteous have been brought to completion.\\f*

\\c 12
\\s1 Run the Race, Looking Away to Jesus
\\p
\\v 1 So then, since we have such a great cloud of witnesses standing around us,\\f + \\fr 12:1 \\ft Witnesses in the sense that their lives gave the evidence — it is the word used all through the last chapter for the approval God spoke over them. He is not picturing a stadium of spectators so much as a ring of sworn testimony.\\f* let us also throw off every weight and the sin that so easily wraps around us,\\f + \\fr 12:1 \\ft "The sin," with the article — a particular one. Given what he has been warning about since the first page, the sin that trips these runners is the letting go: the drifting, the hardening, the shrinking back.\\f* and let us run with endurance the race set before us,
\\v 2 looking away to Jesus, the leader and completer of faithfulness, who for the joy set before him endured a cross, thinking nothing of its shame,\\f + \\fr 12:2 \\ft Crucifixion was not simply a way of killing a man. Rome used it on slaves and rebels precisely because it degraded him in public before it killed him. He did not deny the shame. He counted it as beneath notice.\\f* and has sat down at the right hand of the throne of God.
\\v 3 Think hard about the one who endured such opposition from sinners against himself, so that you do not grow weary and give out inside.
\\s1 The Hardship Is a Father Training Sons
\\p
\\v 4 You have not yet resisted to the point of blood \\add [i.e. it has not yet cost any of you your life]\\add* in your struggle against sin.
\\v 5 And you have forgotten the encouragement that speaks to you as sons: "My son, do not make light of the Lord's discipline, and do not give out when you are corrected by him;
\\v 6 for whom the Lord loves he disciplines, and he flogs every son he receives."\\f + \\fr 12:6 \\ft Proverbs 3:11-12. The word running through this whole passage is the one for bringing a child up — schooling, correction and punishment together, all of it aimed at making the child fit. Israel already read her own wilderness years that way: "as a man disciplines his son, so the LORD your God disciplines you" (Deuteronomy 8:5).\\f*
\\v 7 It is for discipline that you endure. God is dealing with you as with sons; for what son is there whom a father does not discipline?
\\v 8 But if you are without discipline, which all have shared in, then you are illegitimate children \\add [i.e. with no standing in the house and no claim on the inheritance]\\add* and not sons.
\\v 9 Besides, we had the fathers of our flesh to discipline us, and we respected them. Shall we not much more be subject to the Father of spirits and live?
\\v 10 For they disciplined us for a few days as it seemed good to them, but he does it for our profit, so that we may share his holiness.
\\v 11 All discipline, at the time, seems not a matter of joy but of grief; but afterward it gives back the peaceable fruit of righteousness to those who have been trained by it.
\\s1 Straighten Up, and Watch Out for Each Other
\\p
\\v 12 So straighten the hands that hang down and the knees that are giving way,\\f + \\fr 12:12 \\ft Isaiah 35:3, where the same command comes just before the promise that God is on his way to save. Verse 13 borrows from Proverbs 4:26.\\f*
\\v 13 and make straight paths for your feet, so that what is lame may not be pulled out of joint but rather healed \\add [— you have members who are limping already; a crooked path is what will finish them]\\add*.
\\v 14 Chase after peace with everyone, and after the holiness without which no one will see the Lord.
\\v 15 Keep watch that no one falls short of the favor of God, that no root of bitterness springs up and causes trouble and by it many are defiled,\\f + \\fr 12:15 \\ft Deuteronomy 29:18, from the covenant renewal at Moab: the danger is one man or one family inside the community whose heart turns away, and who then bears fruit that poisons the rest.\\f*
\\v 16 that no one is sexually immoral or profane like Esau, who for one meal gave away his birthright.\\f + \\fr 12:16 \\ft Genesis 25:29-34. "Profane" is the opposite of holy — a man who sees nothing sacred anywhere, and so cannot tell an inheritance from a bowl of stew when he is hungry.\\f*
\\v 17 For you know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected; he found no place for a change of mind \\add [i.e. the trade could not be undone]\\add*, though he sought it with tears.\\f + \\fr 12:17 \\ft "And Esau cried out with a great and exceedingly bitter cry" (Genesis 27:34). The tears were real and they changed nothing. That is the whole force of the example: wanting a choice back is not the same as being able to have it back.\\f*
\\s1 You Have Not Come to Sinai but to Zion
\\p
\\v 18 For you have not come to something that can be touched, and to a blazing fire and darkness and gloom and storm,\\f + \\fr 12:18 \\ft The mountain is never named, and every detail names it: Sinai (Exodus 19-20; Deuteronomy 4-5). A mountain you could put your hand on, if putting your hand on it had not been death.\\f*
\\v 19 and the sound of a trumpet and a voice speaking words, so that those who heard it begged that no further word be spoken to them.\\f + \\fr 12:19 \\ft "Do not let God speak to us, or we will die" (Exodus 20:19). Remember that they asked him to stop; verse 25 turns on it.\\f*
\\v 20 For they could not bear the order: "If even an animal touches the mountain, it shall be stoned."
\\v 21 And so terrifying was the sight that Moses said, "I am trembling with fear."\\f + \\fr 12:21 \\ft The words are close to Deuteronomy 9:19 and do not stand in exactly this form in the Exodus account. The point stands either way: the man who went up into the cloud was frightened too.\\f*
\\v 22 But you have come to Mount Zion, to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, to countless thousands of angels,\\f + \\fr 12:22 \\ft Zion is the temple hill in Jerusalem, the place God chose to live: "this is my resting place forever" (Psalm 132:13-14). What they have come to is not the hill in Judea but the original it stood for — the city Abraham was looking for (11:10).\\f*
\\v 23 to a festival gathering and assembly of the firstborn ones \\add [i.e. every one of them holding a firstborn's rights]\\add* enrolled in the heavens, to God the judge of all, to the spirits of righteous ones brought to completion,\\f + \\fr 12:23 \\ft These are the people of the last chapter, who died without receiving what was promised. What was withheld from them then has now been given: they are brought to completion. And a festival gathering is the exact opposite of Sinai — this is a crowd at a feast, not a crowd backing away from a mountain.\\f*
\\v 24 to Jesus the mediator of a new covenant, and to sprinkled blood that speaks something better than the blood of Abel.\\f + \\fr 12:24 \\ft Abel's blood cried out from the ground for justice against the man who spilled it (Genesis 4:10). This blood was spilled too, and what it says is not that.\\f*
\\s1 Do Not Turn Away the One Who Is Speaking
\\p
\\v 25 See that you do not turn away the one who is speaking. For if they did not escape when they turned away the one who warned them on earth, much less will we, if we turn away from the one who warns from heaven.
\\v 26 Then his voice shook the earth, but now he has promised, "Yet once more I will shake not only the earth but also the heaven."\\f + \\fr 12:26 \\ft Haggai 2:6, spoken to men rebuilding a temple that looked like nothing beside the one that had been destroyed. God's answer was that he was not finished shaking things.\\f*
\\v 27 And this "yet once more" makes plain the removal of what is shaken, as of things that have been made, so that what cannot be shaken may remain.
\\v 28 So since we are receiving a kingdom that cannot be shaken, let us hold on to favor, and through it serve God in a way that pleases him, with reverence and awe;
\\v 29 for our God is a consuming fire.\\f + \\fr 12:29 \\ft Deuteronomy 4:24, and in its own place it is a warning to covenant people against breaking the covenant: "take care that you do not forget... for the LORD your God is a consuming fire, a jealous God." The sprinkled blood of verse 24 and the fire of this verse are the same God, and he means both.\\f*

\\c 13
\\s1 How to Live with One Another
\\p
\\v 1 Let brotherly love stay.
\\v 2 Do not forget hospitality to strangers, for through it some have entertained angels without knowing it.\\f + \\fr 13:2 \\ft Abraham at the oaks of Mamre (Genesis 18) and Lot at the gate of Sodom (Genesis 19). Neither of them knew who was at the table until afterward — which is the point. Hospitality is done before you find out what it was worth.\\f*
\\v 3 Remember those in chains as though chained with them, and those who are badly treated, since you also are in a body \\add [i.e. what has happened to them can happen to you]\\add*.
\\v 4 Let marriage be honored among all, and the bed undefiled, for God will judge the sexually immoral and adulterers.
\\v 5 Let your way of life be free of the love of money, and be satisfied with what you have; for he himself has said, "I will never leave you, and I will never abandon you."\\f + \\fr 13:5 \\ft Deuteronomy 31:6 and 8, with Joshua 1:5 — words spoken at the edge of the land, to people who were about to lose the only leader they had ever had. The Greek piles up negatives in a way English cannot: never, not ever, under any circumstances.\\f*
\\v 6 So we can say with confidence, "The Lord is my helper, I will not be afraid. What will man do to me?"\\f + \\fr 13:6 \\ft Psalm 118:6. Not a claim that nothing can be done to you — these people had already been jailed and robbed — but a measuring of what men can do against what God has promised.\\f*
\\s1 Jesus Christ Is the Same, and He Is Outside the Camp
\\p
\\v 7 Remember your leaders, who spoke the word of God to you; look closely at how their way of life came out \\add [i.e. how it ended; you saw the whole of it]\\add*, and imitate their faithfulness.
\\v 8 Jesus Christ is the same yesterday and today and into the ages.\\f + \\fr 13:8 \\ft Placed where it is, this is not a stray doctrinal sentence. The men who taught you are dead; the one they taught you about is not, and has not changed — which is why the next verse can measure new teaching against him.\\f*
\\v 9 Do not be carried off by teachings of every kind and color. For it is good for the heart to be made firm by favor, not by foods \\add [i.e. rules about what may be eaten]\\add*, which have never done any good to those who walk in them.
\\v 10 We have an altar from which those who serve the tent have no right to eat.\\f + \\fr 13:10 \\ft Priests ate their share of most sin offerings (Leviticus 6:26). There was one they never touched: the offering whose blood was carried inside the curtain on the Day of Atonement had its body taken out and burned outside the camp (Leviticus 16:27). That is the offering ours corresponds to, and it is why the men still serving the tent have no portion in it.\\f*
\\v 11 For the bodies of the animals whose blood is brought into the holy places by the high priest for sin are burned outside the camp.
\\v 12 That is why Jesus also suffered outside the gate, so that he might make the people holy through his own blood.\\f + \\fr 13:12 \\ft Outside the wall of Jerusalem (John 19:17, 20) — the city being now what the camp was then. The place of execution was the place of disgrace, and it is where the holiness was accomplished.\\f*
\\v 13 So let us go out to him outside the camp \\add [i.e. out of the safety of the old arrangement, out to where he actually is]\\add*, bearing his reproach.
\\v 14 For here we have no city that stays; we are searching for the one that is coming.
\\v 15 Through him, then, let us bring up to God a sacrifice of praise without stopping, that is, the fruit of lips that confess his name.\\f + \\fr 13:15 \\ft "The fruit of our lips" is Hosea 14:2 in the Greek, where the Hebrew reads "bulls" — words offered by a people who have no altar to bring an animal to. Here it is not a substitute for a sacrifice that is missing. It is what is left to offer once the one sacrifice has been made.\\f*
\\v 16 And do not forget doing good and sharing what you have, for God is pleased with sacrifices like these.
\\s1 Obey Your Leaders, and Pray for Me
\\p
\\v 17 Obey your leaders and give way to them, for they keep awake over your lives as those who will give an account; so let them do this with joy and not with groaning, for that would be no gain to you.
\\v 18 Pray for us, for we are persuaded that we have a good conscience, wanting to conduct ourselves well in every way.
\\v 19 And I urge you all the more to do this, so that I may be given back to you sooner.
\\s1 May the God of Peace Make You Fit
\\p
\\v 20 Now may the God of peace, who brought up from the dead the great shepherd of the sheep by the blood of an everlasting covenant, our Lord Jesus,\\f + \\fr 13:20 \\ft The only place in the whole sermon where the resurrection is stated outright, though it has been assumed on every page. The wording follows Isaiah 63:11 in the Greek, which asks after the God "who brought up from the sea the shepherd of his flock" — Moses out of the Red Sea there, the great shepherd out of death here. And "one shepherd" set over the flock is Ezekiel's promise (Ezekiel 34:23).\\f*
\\v 21 make you fit in every good thing to do his will, working in us what is pleasing before him, through Jesus Christ, to whom be the glory into the ages of the ages. Amen.\\f + \\fr 13:21 \\ft The whole sermon has been telling them what they must do. The prayer at the end asks God to do it in them. He never sets the two against each other.\\f*
\\s1 Final Words
\\p
\\v 22 And I urge you, brothers, bear with this word of encouragement, for I have written to you only briefly.\\f + \\fr 13:22 \\ft "Word of encouragement" was the standing term for the address given in a synagogue after the reading of the Law and the Prophets (Acts 13:15). It is his own name for what he has written: a sermon, not a treatise. "Only briefly," after thirteen chapters, measures the words against the size of the subject.\\f*
\\v 23 Know that our brother Timothy has been released; if he comes soon enough, I will see you with him.
\\v 24 Greet all your leaders and all the holy ones. Those from Italy greet you.\\f + \\fr 13:24 \\ft The phrase most naturally means people from Italy who are away from home, sending greetings back to it — which would put the writer elsewhere and this congregation in or near Rome.\\f*
\\v 25 Favor be with you all.
`;
